WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:07.040
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Today I'm going to show&nbsp;
you the family tree of Chinese emperors. I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.480
mostly be focusing on the medieval and modern&nbsp;
dynasties but before we get to them I am going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.840
to start with the legendary Yellow Emperor and&nbsp;
briefly summarize the dynasties of kings and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.760
emperors who ruled China during ancient times. As&nbsp;
you can see, for most of the video, I'll be using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.680
our brand new East Asian Royal Family Trees chart,&nbsp;
which is available as a poster from our website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.960
UsefulCharts.com. In addition to China, it also&nbsp;
covers Japan (which I've already done a video on)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:43.680
as well as Korea and Mongolia (which I'll be doing&nbsp;
videos on in the future). We also sell two charts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.440
covering the many European royal family trees,&nbsp;
a Timeline of World History, as well as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.640
other titles so, again, if you’re interested&nbsp;
in learning more about any of these posters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:15.280
just head over to UsefulCharts.com or simply click&nbsp;
the link in the description or pinned comment.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.120
Okay, the first thing you need to know&nbsp;
about China is that, unlike Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.760
it has been ruled by many different&nbsp;
dynasties instead of just one. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.600
one of the most important concepts&nbsp;
in Chinese history is the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.440
the Mandate of Heaven—which basically states&nbsp;
that it's normal for one dynasty to overthrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.120
another because whenever that happens, it's&nbsp;
because the old dynasty had become corrupt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.560
therefore Heaven decided that a new dynasty was&nbsp;
needed, in order to restore peace and stability.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:52.080
According to legend, Chinese history begins with&nbsp;
the three sovereigns: Fuxi and Nüwa, who created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.880
humans and Shennong, who invented farming. They&nbsp;
are then followed by five legendary emperors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.320
the first of which, called the Yellow Emperor, is&nbsp;
considered to be the founder of China. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.560
the first dynasty is considered to be the Xia&nbsp;
dynasty, which was founded by Yu the Great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.560
who—according to legend—was the first to control&nbsp;
the floods that occur along the Yellow River. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.480
there is no firm evidence for the Xia dynasty’s&nbsp;
existence and therefore it too is considered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:28.480
be mostly legendary—meaning that Chinese&nbsp;
history really begins around 1600 BCE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.000
with the Shang dynasty—the first set of&nbsp;
monarchs for which we have verifiable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.240
records. But take note, the Shang rulers were not&nbsp;
emperors—they were kings. During their tenure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.880
bronze-working became common in China&nbsp;
and the earliest form of Chinese writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.000
developing (using the Chinese characters&nbsp;
that evolved into the ones still used today).

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.280
But then, in 1046, the Shang dynasty&nbsp;
was overthrown by the Zhou dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:03.040
which can be divided into the initial Western Zhou&nbsp;
and the later Eastern Zhou. You see, in 771 BCE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.840
their capital was overthrown, forcing&nbsp;
them to set up a new capital in the East,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.120
where they continued to reign, but mostly as&nbsp;
just figureheads, with feudal lords holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.320
most of the real power. This coincided with&nbsp;
what’s called the Spring &amp; Autumn Period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.640
which is when the groundwork for Chinese&nbsp;
philosophy was laid, through figures such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.160
as as Lao Tzu, and Confucious. We then get&nbsp;
the Warring States Period, which is when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:33.760
political situation got really chaotic and&nbsp;
the Zhou dynasty formally came to an end.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.360
Which brings us to the actual first emperor&nbsp;
of China: Qin Shi Huang, who managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:46.400
conquer and thus unite all of the Chinese states,&nbsp;
completing the process in 221 BCE. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.480
he upgraded his title of “Wang” (meaning&nbsp;
king) to Huang-di (meaning supreme ruler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.360
or emperor). In addition to being the first&nbsp;
emperor, he is also remembered for starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.680
the construction of the Great Wall of&nbsp;
China and for being buried with an army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:08.800
of 8,000 terracotta soldiers. But for all of his&nbsp;
accomplishments, his dynasty did not last long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:14.640
although the name of the dynasty did, because&nbsp;
it’s from the word Qin that we get the word China.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.040
So, within a decade of the first emperor’s&nbsp;
death, a new dynasty took over: the Han dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.800
founded by Emperor Gaozu. It would go on to&nbsp;
last for about 400 years and is considered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:33.200
be China’s first golden age. It’s most famous&nbsp;
ruler is Emperor Wu, who established the Silk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.840
Road connecting China to India, the Middle&nbsp;
East, and Europe for the first time ever. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.280
descendants ruled until the short-lived Xin&nbsp;
dynasty, when a usurper briefly took over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.040
only to be followed by a restored Han dynasty,&nbsp;
sometimes called the Eastern Han dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:56.320
which lasted until 220 CE. At this point we get&nbsp;
the three kingdoms period, a brief re-unification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.840
under the Jin dynasty, and then a period&nbsp;
known as the Northern and Southern dynasties.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.680
Which bring us to the top of the&nbsp;
East Asian Royal Family Trees poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.240
which I’ll be using for the rest of the video.&nbsp;
After the Northern and Southern dynasties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.800
China was once again re-united by Emperor Wen&nbsp;
of the Sui dynasty. But, as you can see, the Sui&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:23.440
dynasty was short-lived, being quickly replaced&nbsp;
by the Tang dynasty, led by Emperor Gaozu. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:29.040
if you remember, the first emperor of the&nbsp;
earlier Han dynasty was also named Gaozu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.880
This is not a coincidence but rather the result&nbsp;
of the how Chinese emperors are named. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.280
each emperor actually has many different&nbsp;
names, such as their birth name, era name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.720
and temple name, among several others. During&nbsp;
their lifetimes, each emperor would simply have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:50.480
been referred to as Huang-di but after death,&nbsp;
each one was given a special temple name to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:58.000
used for ceremonial purposes. In the case&nbsp;
of both Gaozu of Han and Gaozu of Tang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.080
they were both given the name “Gaozu” because&nbsp;
it literally means “highest ancestor”. So, take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:10.640
note that, until we reach the Ming dynasty, all&nbsp;
emperors will be listed using their temple names.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:17.120
Anyhow, Gaozu was followed by Taizong, who&nbsp;
was given that name because it means “great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.840
ancestor”. What made Taizong so great&nbsp;
was that he was open to following the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.200
advice of his advisors, which led China to&nbsp;
reach new levels of wealth and stability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.240
ushering in a second golden age. In&nbsp;
fact, during his reign, China’s capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:38.640
city (Chang'an) became the largest and most&nbsp;
prosperous city in the entire world and thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.000
most historians today consider Taizong to have&nbsp;
been the greatest Chinese emperor of all time.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.640
He was followed by his son Gaozong, who&nbsp;
ended up falling in love with one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:54.480
father’s lower ranking concubines named Wu&nbsp;
Zetian, eventually making her his official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:02.560
Empress. He however had bad health and eventually&nbsp;
Wu Zetian took over as regent. After Gaozong died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:08.080
Wu Zetian continued to serve as regent&nbsp;
during the first reigns of both Zhongzong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:16.320
and Ruizong. But then in 690, Wu Zetian made&nbsp;
herself emperor—becoming the first, and only,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:22.480
female emperor in Chinese history. Thus, for&nbsp;
15 years, the Tang dynasty was replaced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:29.040
the Wu Zhou dynasty. But eventually, Wu&nbsp;
Zetian was deposed with both Zhongzong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:37.120
and Ruizong once again serving as emperors (as&nbsp;
well as Zhongzong’s son Shang for just 17 days.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:43.040
After all this back-and-forthing, the throne&nbsp;
finally settled on Ruizong’s son Xuanzong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:49.360
who had a nice long reign of 44 years—which is&nbsp;
when the Tang dynasty reached its peak. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.640
he would have reigned even longer had&nbsp;
it not been for the An Lushan Rebellion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.000
which started when a general named An Lushan&nbsp;
tried to overthrow the Tang dynasty. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.880
the initial stages of the rebellion, An Lushan&nbsp;
managed to capture the capital city Chang'an,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:14.000
forcing Emperor Xuanzong to flee. At this point,&nbsp;
Xuanzong’s son Suzong declared himself to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.600
emperor. Suzong managed to retake the capital&nbsp;
but the rebellion wasn’t fully quashed until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:25.920
the first year of his son Daizong’s reign. By&nbsp;
the end, at least 13 million lives were lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.800
making it the deadliest uninterrupted war in human&nbsp;
history, relative to the global population at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:39.040
time. As you can imagine, this put an end to the&nbsp;
Tang dynasty’s golden age and from here onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:45.920
while the dynasty did continue, it was definitely&nbsp;
in decline. The final blow came due to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:52.000
rebellion, the Huang Chao Rebellion, which&nbsp;
occurred during the reign of Xizong. This left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:58.640
China in complete disarray, resulting in the&nbsp;
ultimate collapse of the Tang dynasty in 907.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.880
What followed was the Five Dynasties&nbsp;
and Ten Kingdoms Period during which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.160
time China fractured into a bunch of small&nbsp;
states. But then, as we’ve seen before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.240
re-unification was once again achieved, this&nbsp;
time by the Song Dynasty, initially led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:20.880
Taizu and then by Taizu’s brother Taizong. The&nbsp;
line continued through Taizong with his grandson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.400
Renzong becoming the longest-reigning Song&nbsp;
emperor at 41 years. Now, the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:33.120
thing to note about the Song dynasty period is its&nbsp;
technological achievements, four in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.160
all of which were major improvements on the&nbsp;
four great inventions of ancient China. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.440
first was paper currency. While paper itself&nbsp;
was invented during the Han period and paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:49.280
notes were sometimes used during the Tang period,&nbsp;
it wasn’t until the Song era that the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:55.760
starting issuing official paper currency. The&nbsp;
second achievement was a better compass. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:01.200
the original compass was invented during the Han&nbsp;
period but those were quite heavy and therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:07.520
not really used for navigation. However, during&nbsp;
the Song era, portable compasses were invented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:12.960
meaning that you could take one on a boat&nbsp;
or any long journey. Third, was gunpowder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:18.800
Gunpowder was first formulated during the Tang&nbsp;
period but it wasn’t until the Song era that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:25.760
was used for military purposes. Finally, there was&nbsp;
movable type printing. While whole-page woodblock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:31.040
printing had been used previously, during the&nbsp;
Song era, ceramic tiles for each letter were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:36.560
created so that you could move them around and&nbsp;
re-use them to print whatever text you wanted.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.600
Now, one of the most interesting emperors&nbsp;
of the Song dynasty is Huizong. He was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:47.280
man of the arts—a truly great painter,&nbsp;
poet, and calligrapher—but tragically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:53.040
this meant that he didn’t pay enough attention to&nbsp;
politics and what happened was that a people group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:59.920
to the north (known as the Jurchens) were allowed&nbsp;
to become really powerful. Led by Taizu of Jin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.440
they founded the state of Great Jin, which&nbsp;
eventually attacked Song China. Realizing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:11.920
mistake, Huizong abdicated, passing the throne to&nbsp;
his reluctant son Qinzong. But, within a month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.920
the Great Jin was able to depose Qinzong&nbsp;
and conquer all of Northern China. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.840
Qinzong’s brother, Gaozong, was able to flee&nbsp;
south and set up a new capital there. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:29.920
the Song dynasty was able to continue, but only&nbsp;
in the southern half of the empire. This is why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:35.840
from this point forward, they are labelled as&nbsp;
the Southern Song. Gaozong’s only biological son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:41.520
Zhao Fu, died at age 2, and thus after&nbsp;
Gaozong’s death, the line passed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:48.560
the descendants of the Song dynasty’s founder,&nbsp;
Taizu, first this branch, and then this branch.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:54.240
Now, during the Southern Song period, an even&nbsp;
greater power to the north arose—in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:00.880
one of the greatest that the world has ever&nbsp;
seen: the Mongols, led by Genghis Khan. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:06.720
he himself wasn’t able to conquer China during&nbsp;
his lifetime, the Jin Dynasty ended up falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:13.520
to his son Ögedei Khan; and the Southern Song to&nbsp;
his grandson Kublai Khan. It was during this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:22.960
period that Marco Polo famously visited China. In&nbsp;
1279, Khan became the undisputed Emperor of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:29.360
and from here onwards he and descendants are&nbsp;
known as the Yuan dynasty of China. After death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:35.760
Kublai Khan received the temple name Shizu, with&nbsp;
the next emperor being his grandson Chengzong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.720
But then, after Chengzong, things bounced&nbsp;
back forth between various branches of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:48.880
tree before finally settling on Huizong, who had&nbsp;
a nice long reign of 35 years. However, in 1368,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:56.800
after 89 years of Mongol-rule, China won back its&nbsp;
independence with the rise of the Ming dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:02.640
initially led by the Hongwu Emperor. At&nbsp;
this point, the Yuan dynasty did continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:08.880
but only to the north of China, which is why&nbsp;
we get a label change here to Northern Yuan.

