00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:49.840
Okay, let’s now take a look at the Ming&nbsp;
dynasty. First off, as I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:56.000
from here onwards, era names will be used for&nbsp;
emperors instead of temple names (as this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:01.200
convention used by most historians). Second,&nbsp;
take note that it was during the Ming period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:07.520
that Beijing as we know it today started to be&nbsp;
built, on top of what had previously been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:13.760
Yuan capital. This included famous structures&nbsp;
such as the Forbidden City, Tiananmen gate, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:18.960
Temple of Heaven—all of which were commissioned&nbsp;
by the Yongle emperor, who is considered to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:25.680
been the greatest emperor of the Ming dynasty.&nbsp;
You see, it was he who named Beijing “bei jing”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.840
which literally just means “Northern capital”. The&nbsp;
Yongle emperor is also the one who commissioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:38.720
the great explorer Zheng He, whose famous treasure&nbsp;
fleet travelled all over Southeast Asia, India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:44.160
and the Middle East—even going as far&nbsp;
as what is today Kenya, in East Africa.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:49.120
The other thing that comes to mind when people&nbsp;
think about the Ming dynasty is china. Not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:55.280
country itself but the type of ceramic known as&nbsp;
porcelain or “fine china” (which was obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:59.680
named after the country). You see, during&nbsp;
the reign of the Xuande emperor, a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:06.080
type of blue and white porcelain from a Chinese&nbsp;
city called Jingdezhen became really popular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:12.000
and it remains popular to this day, all over&nbsp;
the world. Other Ming rulers of note include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:18.320
the Hongzhi Emperor (who was the only Chinese&nbsp;
emperor to have just one wife) and the Wanli&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:23.120
Emperor, who set the stage for the dynasty’s&nbsp;
decline when, part way through his reign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:29.120
he refused to perform any of his duties. His&nbsp;
final few years as emperor coincided with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:35.760
rise of the later Jin dynasty in the north. They&nbsp;
were led by Nurhaci, who had managed to re-unite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:42.240
the Jurchen tribes—with the aim of conquering&nbsp;
China like the earlier Jin dynasty had done.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:47.440
Nurhaci was followed by his son Hong&nbsp;
Taiji, who conquered Inner Mongolia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:53.520
putting an end to the NorthernYuan dynasty. Around&nbsp;
the same time, he changed the name of the Jurchen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:59.520
people to the Manchu people—which is where the&nbsp;
word Manchuria comes from. He also changed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:05.520
name of the Jin dynasty to the Qing Dynasty,&nbsp;
because the Chinese letter for Qing contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:12.240
the water radical; whereas the letter for Ming&nbsp;
contains the fire radical. Water puts out fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:17.200
so, in other words, he was saying: the&nbsp;
Qing are about to extinguish the Ming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:22.560
And he was right because just after he&nbsp;
died, his half-brother Dorgon (serving as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:30.320
regent for Hong Tai-ji’s son Shunzhi) did&nbsp;
exactly that, capturing Beijing in 1644.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:36.560
Now, when Beijing fell, a cousin of the last&nbsp;
Ming emperor fled south and from there declared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:41.680
himself to be the true emperor. He was followed&nbsp;
in quick succession by three other members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:48.520
Ming dynasty (together they are known as the&nbsp;
“Southern Ming” dynasty). In 1662 though, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:55.600
Qing conquered the Southern Ming as well, forcing&nbsp;
the final Ming loyalists to flee to Taiwan, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.600 --> 00:18:01.040
they took over the island from the Dutch and set&nbsp;
up the Kingdom of Tungning. However, that kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:07.440
only lasted about 20 years and eventually they&nbsp;
surrendered to the [ching] Qing as well, in 1681.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:12.880
By this point, the Kangxi emperor was on the&nbsp;
throne. He ended up being the longest-reigning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:19.760
Chinese emperor of all-time, at 61 years, and&nbsp;
during his reign China once again became stable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:26.480
and prosperous. He was followed by his son,&nbsp;
and then by his grandson, the Qianlong emperor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:31.760
who also had a really long reign. In fact, he&nbsp;
would have beat the Kangxi emperor’s record but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:37.040
opted to retire after 60 years, out of&nbsp;
respect for his grandfather. During his reign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:41.920
China reached its greatest extent,&nbsp;
as you can see from this map here.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:47.760
During the 19th century however, the Qing&nbsp;
Empire started to decline, especially after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:53.040
losing the two Opium Wars against the United&nbsp;
Kingdom, which led to the creation of Hong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:58.960
Kong as a British colony. At the same time&nbsp;
as the Second Opium War, China was also in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:05.840
the middle of an internal conflict, known as the&nbsp;
Taiping Rebellion. It started when this man—Hong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:13.520
Xiuquan—claimed to be the younger brother of, wait&nbsp;
for it, Jesus. Hong Xiuquan managed to set up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:20.000
Taiping Heavenly Kingdom, centered at Nanjing,&nbsp;
which lasted for about 14 years. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:26.480
the Qing armies were able to quash the&nbsp;
rebellion but at a great cost to human life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:33.680
Estimates put the death count at around 20-30&nbsp;
million, which is more than that of World War I.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:40.560
Now, during this tumultuous period, the Xianfeng&nbsp;
emperor died from poor health at age 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:46.960
leading his 5-year old son to become the&nbsp;
Tongzhi emperor. Soon thereafter, his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:53.680
Empress Cixi staged a coup, placing herself in&nbsp;
the position of regent, so it was actually she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:58.880
who was in charge at the end of the Taiping&nbsp;
Rebellion. She ended up ruling much longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:05.440
than expected though because, just as the Tongzhi&nbsp;
emperor reached adulthood, he died from smallpox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:12.880
at which point Cixi made her 3-year old nephew&nbsp;
the Guangxu emperor so that she could continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:18.400
be regent. Even when the Guangxu emperor reached&nbsp;
adulthood, she continued to be the power behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:24.160
the throne and therefore ended up serving as&nbsp;
the de facto ruler of China for a total of 47&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:30.240
years. Her last political move, while on her&nbsp;
deathbed, was to arrange the assassination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:36.000
the Guangxu emperor by way of arsenic poisoning.&nbsp;
Basically, she didn’t want him reversing any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:42.000
her policies after her death. As it turned out,&nbsp;
she actually died just one day after her nephew.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:47.840
With both main members of the imperial family&nbsp;
now dead, her allies followed her wishes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:54.400
made her two-year old great-nephew Puyi emperor&nbsp;
so that they could continue to be in control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.400 --> 00:21:00.320
But by this point, Imperial China had been&nbsp;
further weakened by wars against France, Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:06.960
and then eight nations all at once during the&nbsp;
Boxer Rebellion. In 1911, a revolution broke out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:13.280
and by February the next year, Puyi was forced&nbsp;
to abdicate, bringing an end to the 4000-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:20.160
monarchy of China. At first, China became the&nbsp;
Republic of China but then, in 1949, as I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:26.240
you know, it became the communist-led People’s&nbsp;
Republic of China, which is what it is today.

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:32.160
Now, before I go, I do want to address the&nbsp;
question: Who would be emperor of China today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:38.560
if, for some reason, China wanted to restore its&nbsp;
monarchy or if the Qing dynasty had never been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:44.880
deposed? Well, first off, I want to point out&nbsp;
thatPuyi did actually reign as emperor again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:50.880
this time as an adult but also as a puppet of&nbsp;
Japan during the years that they controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:56.640
Manchuria, which they called Manchu-kua.&nbsp;
After World War II, he was held as a prisoner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:02.000
first in the Soviet Union, and then later in&nbsp;
Communist China, before finally being released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:09.440
in 1959 to live out his final 8 years as a regular&nbsp;
citizen (although, obviously, he remained under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:15.360
close watch). Puyi did marry five times but&nbsp;
never had any children so after his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:21.440
his claim to the imperial throne passed, first to&nbsp;
his full-brother [poo-jyeh] Pujie (who had only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:27.600
daughters) and then to his half-brother Pu-ren,&nbsp;
better known as Jin [yo-juh] Youzhi. Surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:32.400
he lived all the way to 2015, at which&nbsp;
point the claim passed to his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:38.240
Jin [yoo-jahng] Yuzhang. So it is this man&nbsp;
here who would be emperor of China today.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:43.840
Okay, so that was a look at the family&nbsp;
tree of Chinese emperors. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:47.520
if you want to get a copy of this&nbsp;
chart as a poster, just head over to&nbsp;&nbsp;

